   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Disability equity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia assisted 67 organisations for people with disabilities (OPDs) to improve how national and sub-national planning and budgeting processes support the delivery of appropriate basic services to people with disabilities. 
&#x200d;
OPDs are supported to enable them contributing to provincial annual, medium-term, and long-term development planning.  OPDs are provided with the skills and opportunity to analyse local data on people with disabilities so they are able to identify and advocate for their own priorities in the provision of basic services. This provided OPDs with the platform to participate in drafting provincial regulations on gender mainstreaming and disability and Provincial Action Plans for disability. 
&#x200d;
Australia supported OPDs on improving how, and under what categories, the National Planning Ministry requires provinces to allocate budget for the support of PWD.  The improved nomenclature and responsiveness to local priorities will facilitate more inclusive and appropriate programming in provinces and districts through to 2045.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia supported the Ministry of Finance (MoF) to integrate the National Gender Mainstreaming Strategy into laws governing all fiscal transfers from central to regional governments. This involved supporting the design and testing of 1) a model for tagging transfers; 2) appropriate categories to guide transfer use and 3) a system to monitor the integration process and outcomes. This enhances MoF&#x2019;s ability to hold regional governments to account for the use of fiscal transfers in ways aligned with the National Strategy. 
&#x200d;
The first round of monitoring highlighted that greater clarity is needed on how the National Strategy objectives relate to transfer use and expected outcomes for MoF to effectively monitor the outcomes of the tagging and use of fiscal transfers.  Australia is now supporting MoF to respond to these monitoring insights in the revision of the fiscal transfer laws and accompanying monitoring system, prior to rolling out the model nationally.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved Governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">SKALA and INKLUSI supported the East Nusa Tenggara (NTT) Provincial Government in developing the Regional Action Plan on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities (RAD PD) 2024&#x2013;2026&#x2014;a key document for advancing disability inclusion. Leveraging INKLUSI&#x2019;s early advocacy, SKALA provided technical guidance, capacity building, partnership brokering, and high-level political advocacy.
&#x200d;
Significantly, meaningful participation was prioritised, with 7 organisations of persons with disabilities facilitating the drafting process and a further 18 engaged in participatory analysis to identify gaps in basic rights and services. SKALA also facilitated substantive dialogue with the National Development Planning Agency (Bappenas) to align the RAD PD with national frameworks.
&#x200d;
The NTT RAD PD is one of only five developed out of 39 possible provinces, positioning NTT as a leader in inclusive governance. As a result of effective collaboration between DFAT programs and thorough monitoring and evaluation of the process and product, the experience of the provincial NTT government presents a valuable learning case for other provinces. &#x200d;
With the 2025&#x2013;2029 National Action Plan on Disabilities (RAN PD) expected soon, the experience from NTT - and similar support provided by SKALA in Aceh - has the potential to inform national best-practice guidelines for disability-inclusive planning across Indonesia.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Disability equity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">SKALA provides support to coalitions of civil society organisations (CSO) to contribute meaningfully to public decision making. As part of this effort, SKALA supported 45 organisations of persons with a disability (OPDs) to work together to inform the development of key public policies and regulations. By adopting a strategy of learning-by-doing, providing learning sessions to familiarise the organisations with government processes and supporting them to prepare evidenced recommendations to government, SKALA was able to improve their capacity to engage in government planning processes. Through this mechanism 9 OPDs receive capacity building/learning on data analysis; 15 on government processes; and 21 had their capacity built in both data and government processes.
&#x200d;
In order to support the institutional capacity of CSO Networks, in Maluku province, SKALA supported the Maluku Deaf Community to legally become part of the Gerkatin organisation (Indonesian Deaf Welfare Movement). SKALA facilitated the discussions between members, supported the preparation of the requirements needed to become a Gerkatin organisation. With the transformation of the Maluku Deaf Community into a Gerkatin organisation, the advocacy of the organisation becomes stronger in the eyes of the law.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
